  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe proposed approach aims to improve upon the previous simulated annealing method by introducing a more robust optimization framework. The key insights are:\n\n- The problem is non-convex due to the product of h and (1 - h) in the overlap integral, necessitating a careful balance between exploration and exploitation.\n\n- A more effective repair mechanism is essential to maintain feasibility while exploring the solution space.\n\n- Leveraging convex optimization techniques for repair steps can enhance the robustness of the solution.\n\nThe strategy combines gradient-based optimization with a carefully designed random perturbation scheme, ensuring that feasibility is maintained through quadratic projections.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nDefine h as a vector of variables h \u2208 [0,1]^n, where n = n_points. The objective is:\n\nC\u2085 = max_{k} (\u2211_{i=0}^{n-|k|} h[i](1 - h[i+|k|]) dx )\n\nSubject to:\n\n\u2211 h[i] dx = 1, h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n\nTo find the minimal C\u2085, the optimization must:\n\na) Use gradient descent with adaptive steps to explore the solution space.\n\nb) Repair infeasible solutions via projection onto [0,1]^n and the hyperplane \u2211h = n/2.\n\nc) Sample multiple initializations and adapt perturbation rates based on progress.\n\nThe repair projection solves:\n\nmin ||h_new - h_perturbed||\u00b2  \ns.t. 0 \u2264 h_new[i] \u2264 1, \u2211h_new[i] = n/2\n\nThis ensures feasibility without oscillations.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\na) Initialization:\n\n1. Start with initial_h_values or random initialization.\n2. Project to feasible set using QP repair.\n\nb) Optimization Loop:\n\n1. Compute gradient of C\u2085 w.r. to h via finite differences.\n2. Update h using adaptive gradient descent.\n3. Apply random perturbations to h elements proportional to 1/T.\n4. Repair h using QP projection.\n5. Track best h and update patience if no improvement.\n\nc) Termination:\n\n1. Stop after budget_s or no improvement for 1000 steps.\n2. Final repair and evaluation.\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n\n- n_points: 100 (as in initial solution)\n- Initial step size: 0.01, adaptive based on progress\n- Perturbation rate: Start at 0.05, adaptive\n- QP repair tolerance: 1e-6\n- Max iterations: 20000, adaptive based on budget\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\nEach QP repair takes ~0.1s. With 20000 iterations, total time is ~2000s. However, only 600s budget, so adaptively reduce steps or tolerance if needed.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\n- Ensure h \u2208 [0,1]^n and \u2211h = n/2 via QP.\n- Evaluate final C\u2085 using np.correlate.\n- Return h, C\u2085, and n_points as specified.",
